{"intent":"build","plan":"Place an Arduino Uno, four LEDs with 220 ohm current-limiting resistors, a tactile button, and a potentiometer. Wire the components to digital and analog pins on the Uno, and power them via the 5V and GND pins. Upload an Arduino sketch to scan through the LEDs, controlled by the button and pot.","reply":"I have wired an Arduino Uno with 4 LEDs, 220Ω resistors, a tactile button, and a trimpot knob. The sketch scans the LEDs, with speed or behavior modulated by the analog input and button.","more":false,"clearExisting":true,"board":"half","parts":[{"ref":"V1","type":"vcc","props":{}},{"ref":"G1","type":"gnd","props":{}},{"ref":"U1","type":"uno","props":{"code":"const int ledPins[] = {2, 3, 4, 5};
const int btnPin = 6;
const int potPin = A0;
int currentLed = 0;
int lastBtnState = HIGH;

void setup() {
  for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    pinMode(ledPins[i], OUTPUT);
  }
  pinMode(btnPin, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int potVal = analogRead(potPin);
  int delayTime = map(potVal, 0, 1023, 50, 500);

  int btnState = digitalRead(btnPin);
  if (btnState == LOW && lastBtnState == HIGH) {
    currentLed = (currentLed + 1) % 4;
    delay(50);
  }
  lastBtnState = btnState;

  for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    digitalWrite(ledPins[i], i == currentLed ? HIGH : LOW);
  }
  delay(delayTime);
}"}},{"ref":"L1","type":"led","props":{"color":"red","protect":"auto"}},{"ref":"L2","type":"led","props":{"color":"yellow","protect":"auto"}},{"ref":"L3","type":"led","props":{"color":"green","protect":"auto"}},{"ref":"L4","type":"led","props":{"color":"blue","protect":"auto"}},{"ref":"R1","type":"resistor","props":{"ohms":220}},{"ref":"R2","type":"resistor","props":{"ohms":220}},{"ref":"R3","type":"resistor","props":{"ohms":220}},{"ref":"R4","type":"resistor","props":{"ohms":220}},{"ref":"B1","type":"tactile","props":{"bounce":true}},{"ref":"P1","type":"pot","props":{"ohms":10000}}],"connections":[["V1.1","+"],["G1.1","-"],["U1.5V","+"],["U1.GND","-"],["U1.D2","R1.1"],["R1.2","L1.Anode (+)"],["L1.Cathode (−)","-"],["U1.D3","R2.1"],["R2.2","L2.Anode (+)"],["L2.Cathode (−)","-"],["U1.D4","R3.1"],["R3.2","L3.Anode (+)"],["L3.Cathode (−)","-"],["U1.D5","R4.1"],["R4.2","L4.Anode (+)"],["L4.Cathode (−)","-"],["U1.D6","B1.1.l"],["B1.1.r","-"],["U1.A0","P1.Wiper"],["P1.1","+"],["P1.3","-"]]}

{"intent":"build","plan":"Create a Simon Says game using an Arduino Uno, 4 colored LEDs with current-limiting resistors, 4 pushbutton switches, and a buzzer.","reply":"Here is your Simon Says game circuit built with an Arduino Uno, 4 colored LEDs, 4 pushbuttons, and a buzzer!","more":false,"clearExisting":true,"board":"half","parts":[{"ref":"U1","type":"uno","props":{"code":"const int ledPins[] = {2, 3, 4, 5};
const int btnPins[] = {6, 7, 8, 9};
const int buzzerPin = 10;
int sequence[100];
int gameLen = 0;
void setup() {
  for(int i=0; i<4; i++) {
    pinMode(ledPins[i], OUTPUT);
    pinMode(btnPins[i], INPUT_PULLUP);
  }
  pinMode(buzzerPin, OUTPUT);
  randomSeed(analogRead(0));
}
void loop() {
  sequence[gameLen++] = random(0, 4);
  for(int i=0; i<gameLen; i++) {
    digitalWrite(ledPins[sequence[i]], HIGH);
    tone(buzzerPin, 200 + sequence[i]*100, 200);
    delay(300);
    digitalWrite(ledPins[sequence[i]], LOW);
    delay(200);
  }
  for(int i=0; i<gameLen; i++) {
    int pressed = -1;
    while(pressed == -1) {
      for(int j=0; j<4; j++) {
        if(digitalRead(btnPins[j]) == LOW) {
          pressed = j;
          digitalWrite(ledPins[j], HIGH);
          tone(buzzerPin, 200 + j*100, 200);
          delay(200);
          digitalWrite(ledPins[j], LOW);
          while(digitalRead(btnPins[j]) == LOW);
        }
      }
    }
    if(pressed != sequence[i]) {
      for(int k=0; k<3; k++) {
        tone(buzzerPin, 100, 100);
        delay(150);
      }
      gameLen = 0;
      delay(1000);
      break;
    }
